# Review routing (§12.4, #58).
#
# See docs/ownership.md for the model this encodes, why the split is drawn
# where it is, and how to become a maintainer for an area.
#
# ## LAST match wins — not first, and not most specific
#
# This is the opposite of .gitignore and of most people's intuition, and it is
# the single easiest way to get this file wrong. The catch-all is therefore
# FIRST and the specific areas follow, so an area rule always overrides it. Add
# new rules BELOW the ones they should beat.
#
# ## Every owner must have write access
#
# GitHub silently ignores a CODEOWNERS entry naming a user or team without
# write access to the repository — no error, no warning, the rule simply never
# routes. If a handle below stops being able to review, its area quietly loses
# its owner. `.github/scripts/check-codeowners.mjs` catches dead PATTERNS; it
# cannot check permissions, which are not visible from the repository.
#
# ## Today, every area routes to both owners
#
# @didi178 and @alprimak co-own every path, by founder decision on 2026-09-08
# (#647). §12.4's boundaries were drawn before the people existed so that adding
# a maintainer would be a one-line change to an existing boundary rather than an
# argument about where the boundary goes — and this is that change.
#
# BOTH HANDLES ARE ON EVERY RULE, not only the catch-all, and that is
# load-bearing rather than repetitive. LAST match wins, so a second owner added
# to `*` alone would be overridden by every area rule beneath it, and every path
# except the ones no area names would still route to one person. The areas stay
# listed separately for the original reason: handing one to a different
# maintainer remains a one-line edit.
#
# ## What this file does and does not gate today (#330, #647)
#
# The repository is public and a branch ruleset requires 1 code-owner approval,
# so these rules are honoured by GitHub — where previously the plan tier meant
# they were not. Two independent reasons, either alone sufficient, meant they
# gated NO PR authored here. The founder's decision of 2026-09-08 resolves the
# first and deliberately keeps the second:
#
#   1. SELF-AUTHORSHIP — RESOLVED BY THIS FILE. GitHub never requests review
#      from a PR's own author, and every path used to be owned by the one
#      account that authors every PR. A second owner now exists, so a
#      @didi178-authored PR routes review to @alprimak. This is the first time
#      these rules route anything.
#   2. BYPASS — STILL HOLDS, AND IS SUFFICIENT ALONE. @didi178 remains an
#      `always` bypass actor on both rulesets, as are organisation admins and
#      the admin role. The rule does not apply to the account that authors and
#      merges, even now that an approver exists.
#
# So code-owner approval here is REQUESTABLE, NOT REQUIRED. Removing the bypass
# entry is what would fix (2); it was considered and DECLINED in the same
# decision, in exchange for keeping merge throughput — not overlooked.
#
# A second REVIEWER now exists; a second NON-BYPASSING reviewer does not, and
# the distinction is the whole content of (2). @alprimak holds `admin`, so they
# are additionally covered by the OrganizationAdmin and admin-RepositoryRole
# bypass entries the same decision retains.
#
# So this file is no longer only STRUCTURE FOR FUTURE MAINTAINERS — it now
# routes a review request — but it is still not a control on today's changes.
#
# Deliberately no status word. "Dormant" and "live" are each one platform
# setting away from being false, and the claim this replaces went stale without
# anyone touching the file — which is what #330 was filed about. A condition can
# be re-checked; a status can only be believed.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Default
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
*                                   @didi178 @alprimak

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# §12.4 boundaries
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# core/ — core maintainers.
# The compiler, dispatcher, policy engine, presets, audit and redaction. Every
# other package depends on it, so a change here reaches everything.
/packages/core/                     @didi178 @alprimak

# transports/ — protocol maintainers.
# The MCP wire contract. Changes here are visible to every client that speaks
# to us, including ones we do not control.
/packages/transports/               @didi178 @alprimak

# sources/openapi/ — schema maintainers.
/packages/sources-openapi/          @didi178 @alprimak

# adapters/ — adapter owners, AND a green conformance run.
# The conformance requirement is a REVIEW rule, not something CODEOWNERS can
# express; see docs/ownership.md. CI enforces the run, an owner enforces the
# judgement, and neither substitutes for the other.
/packages/adapters-express/         @didi178 @alprimak
/packages/adapters-fastify/         @didi178 @alprimak

# explorer/ — UI maintainers.
/packages/explorer/                 @didi178 @alprimak

# observability/ — runtime/SRE maintainers.
/packages/observability/            @didi178 @alprimak
/packages/reliability/              @didi178 @alprimak

# examples/ — shared ownership.
# The lightest boundary in the file, deliberately: examples are how people
# start, and a heavy review burden on them is how they go stale.
/examples/                          @didi178 @alprimak
/packages/examples/                 @didi178 @alprimak

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Areas §12.4 predates
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# The conformance bank and the kit that runs it.
#
# Held SEPARATELY from adapters/ on purpose. These define the contract that
# gates adapter contributions, so an adapter owner who also owned the bank
# could weaken the gate governing their own work. That is moot while one person
# owns everything, and it is exactly the kind of thing that is impossible to
# retrofit once several people do — see docs/ownership.md § Separation of duties.
/packages/adapter-conformance/      @didi178 @alprimak
/packages/adapter-test/             @didi178 @alprimak

# Standalone gateway. A deployment artifact — a container and a published
# binary — rather than a library, so it carries release-surface risk the
# embedded packages do not.
/packages/gateway/                  @didi178 @alprimak

# Operator tooling: doctor, inspect, diff, diagnostics.
/packages/cli/                      @didi178 @alprimak

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Repository infrastructure
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# CI configuration and the guard scripts.
#
# Listed explicitly because these are the gates every other rule relies on. A
# change here can disable a check for the whole repository, which is a larger
# blast radius than any single package.
/.github/                           @didi178 @alprimak

# Merge behaviour for the append-only audit logs (#128). A change here can
# reintroduce silent row loss.
/.gitattributes                     @didi178 @alprimak

# Contracts the project has published and owes people stability on.
/docs/compatibility.md              @didi178 @alprimak
/docs/generated-output-license.md   @didi178 @alprimak
/docs/telemetry-policy.md           @didi178 @alprimak
/docs/ownership.md                  @didi178 @alprimak
/LICENSE                            @didi178 @alprimak
/NOTICE                             @didi178 @alprimak

# The Factor 2 concealment allowlist (#276).
#
# This decides which instruction-shaped messages an agent may treat as routine,
# so widening it is a security change. It is also the one security control here
# that lives in an UNPROTECTED path — `.operum/audit/` is engineer-owned, so any
# agent can edit it in an ordinary PR.
#
# check-concealment-templates.mjs enforces that every template is backed by a
# captured message, which makes a widening diff loud. It cannot judge whether
# the widening SHOULD happen. That is what this rule is for.
#
# Listed last because the last matching rule wins.
/.operum/audit/concealment-templates.toml  @didi178 @alprimak
